"Z. Wang, Y. Zhang / Filomat 39:33 (2025), 11961–11967                                                                 11962 (https://www.pmf.ni.ac.rs/filomat-content/2025/39-33/39-33-22-27362.pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; For results on the Ramsey numbers of wheels, see Faudree and McKay [11] (which disproved a conjecture of Erdős), Lidický and Pfender [14], Van Overberghe [22], and others. ... For the Ramsey numbers of odd cycles versus large wheels, Zhang, Zhang, and Chen [27], Sanhueza-Matamala [17], and Alweiss [1] have obtained a series of results. ... When t = 1, this result coincides with the classical cycle-wheel Ramsey number\nFor results on the Ramsey numbers of wheels, see Faudree and McKay [11] (which disproved a conjecture of Erdős), Lidický and Pfender [14], Van Overberghe [22], and others.\n\nThe Ramsey numbers for cycles versus wheels were first studied by Burr and Erdős [7], where they proved that R(C₃, W_ℓ) = 2ℓ + 1 for ℓ ≥ 5. However, when C₃ is replaced by C₄, the complexity of the problem increases significantly. Zhang, Broersma, and Chen [25] proved that R(C₄, W_ℓ) = R(C₄, K_{1,ℓ}) for ℓ ≥ 6. The exact value of the latter is currently known only in certain special cases, and an upper bound problem in its general case includes a 100-dollar conjecture of Erdős [8].\n\nFor the Ramsey numbers of odd cycles versus large wheels, Zhang, Zhang, and Chen [27], Sanhueza-Matamala [17], and Alweiss [1] have obtained a series of results. For even cycles and even-order wheels, when n < ℓ < 3n/2, Zhang, Broersma, and Chen [26] also determined the exact value of R(C_n, W_ℓ).\n\nWhen the cycle is relatively large compared to the wheel, the parity of the wheel plays a crucial role in its Ramsey number. Chen, Cheng, Miao, and Ng [9] extended the range of n to n ≥ 3m + 1. Zhang, Broersma, and Chen [26] further extended the range of n to n ≥ 2m + 502. Raeisi and Zaghian [15] independently extended the range of n to n ≥ 2m + 500, requiring n to be even. Combining these results, the best known result to date states that for m ≥ 2 and n ≥ 2m + min{m + 1, 502}, the equality holds.\n\nSudarasana [19] further generalized this problem by introducing multiple wheels. What happens if the five-vertex wheel W₄ is replaced by t pairwise disjoint copies of W₄? Sudarsana obtained the following theorem.\n\n**Theorem 1.1 (Sudarsana [19]).** R(C_n, tW₄) = 2n + t − 2 for n ≥ 15t² − 4t + 2.\n\nSudarsana [19] also proposed the following conjecture.\n\n**Conjecture 1.2.** For t ≥ 1 and m ≥ 2, there exists a number n₀ such that for n ≥ n₀,\n\nR(C_n, tW_{2m}) = 2n + t − 2 and R(C_n, tW_{2m+1}) = 3n + t − 3.\n\nThe main theorem of this paper confirms Conjecture 1.2 for odd-order wheels.\n\n**Theorem 1.3.** For t ≥ 1 and m ≥ 2, when n ≥ 4tm, we have R(C_n, tW_{2m}) = 2n + t − 2.\n\nWhen m = 2, the above theorem states that as long as n ≥ 8t, the equality holds. This result significantly improves Theorem 1.1. When t = 1, this result coincides with the classical cycle-wheel Ramsey number--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRamsey numbers of odd cycles versus larger even wheels - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2018.01.004)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Note that this proves the conjecture of Zhang, Zhang, and Chen in almost all cases. ... In the next section, we will then use one of the classical theorems, due to Fan [6], as well as bounds on Ramsey numbers to find a large red cycle. ... If all vertices of a non-bipartite 2$2$-connected graph on n$n$ vertices have degree at least $\\frac{n}{4}+250$, the graph has all cycles lengths between 6$6$ and $c\\left(G\\right)$.\n## [11]\n\nFor $k<j<3k∕2$, $R({C}_{2k+1},{W}_{2j})=4j+1$.\n\nA graph with $4j$ vertices consisting of two disjoint blue copies of {K}_{2j}${K}_{2j}$ with all red edges between them lacks both a red {C}_{2k+1}${C}_{2k+1}$ and a blue {W}_{2j}${W}_{2j}$. Thus $4j+1